/-!
# Separable and separably generated field extensions

Let `K/k` be a field extension. We say `K` is *separably generated over `k`* if there is a
transcendence basis `{xᵢ; i ∈ I}` of `K/k` such that `K` is separable algebraic over the purely
transcendental subextension `k(xᵢ; i ∈ I)`, and we say `K` is *separable over `k`* if every
subextension `k ⊆ K' ⊆ K` with `K'` finitely generated over `k` is separably generated over `k`.
For an algebraic extension the only transcendence basis is the empty one, so both notions say in
that case that `K` is separable algebraic over `k`; for transcendental extensions they are weaker,
`k(x)/k` being separably generated by the basis `{x}`. In characteristic zero every extension is
separable, whereas `𝐅_p(t)(t^{1/p})` is not separable over `𝐅_p(t)`.

## Main definitions

* `Algebra.IsSeparablyGenerated` (Mathlib's, `Mathlib.FieldTheory.TranscendentalSeparable`): the
  extension `K/k` has a transcendence basis `s` such that `K` is separable algebraic over `k(s)`.
* `Algebra.IsSeparableExtension`: every subextension of `K/k` that is finitely generated over `k`
  is separably generated over `k`.

## Implementation notes

The transcendence basis is a subset `s : Set K` rather than an indexed family `x : ι → K`. A
transcendence basis is injective, so no generality is lost.

"Separable algebraic" is `Algebra.IsSeparable`: separability of the minimal polynomial of every
element already forces integrality, and algebraicity of `K` over `k(s)` is in any case automatic
from `IsTranscendenceBasis`.

Finite generation of a subextension `K'` over `k` is `Algebra.EssFiniteType k K'`, Mathlib's
spelling of it for field extensions: it is equivalent to finite generation of `K'` as an
intermediate field of `K/k` (`IntermediateField.essFiniteType_iff`) and to
`(⊤ : IntermediateField k K').FG` (`IntermediateField.fg_top_iff`). The projection
`Algebra.IsSeparableExtension.isSeparablyGenerated` is an instance.

Mathlib's `Algebra.IsSeparable` is separability of an algebraic extension; the notion here, which
does not require algebraicity, is named `Algebra.IsSeparableExtension`.

## References

* [Stacks 030O](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/030O)
-/

@[expose] public section

@[expose] public section

namespace Algebra

attribute [sga "1 II 5.6"] IsSeparablyGenerated

/-- A field extension `K/k` is *separable* if every subextension `k ⊆ K' ⊆ K` with `K'` finitely
generated over `k` is separably generated over `k`. For algebraic extensions this is Mathlib's
`Algebra.IsSeparable`. -/
@[stacks 030O "part (2)"]
public class IsSeparableExtension (k K : Type*) [Field k] [Field K] [Algebra k K] : Prop where
  /-- Every subextension of `K/k` that is finitely generated over `k` is separably generated. -/
  isSeparablyGenerated (K' : IntermediateField k K) [Algebra.EssFiniteType k K'] :
    IsSeparablyGenerated k K'

attribute [instance] IsSeparableExtension.isSeparablyGenerated

end Algebra

/- `Algebra.IsSeparableExtension` is equivalent to its single field:
`Algebra.isSeparableExtension_iff` (Mathlib's `@[mk_iff]` gives
`Algebra.isSeparablyGenerated_iff`). -/
mk_iff_of_inductive_prop Algebra.IsSeparableExtension Algebra.isSeparableExtension_iff

end
